## Problem

Dartmouth students, specifically vegans and vegetarians, experienced Dartmouth Dining as fragmented—nutrition information, hours, and dietary needs were hard to act on in the moment.

I ran a mixed-methods study: competitive analysis, a 21-response population survey, and a five-day diary study with 14 participants over ten weeks. I synthesized with statistical testing, BERT sentiment analysis, and TF-IDF topic extraction.

Findings supported a dining app feature set and nutrition-focused open office hours with the Dartmouth Dining dietitian. Both were implemented by Dartmouth Dining.

## Process

I ran a mixed-methods study: competitive analysis, a 21-response population survey, and a five-day diary study with 14 participants over ten weeks. I synthesized with statistical testing, BERT sentiment analysis, and TF-IDF topic extraction.

## Outcome

Findings supported a dining app feature set and nutrition-focused open office hours with the Dartmouth Dining dietitian. Both were implemented by Dartmouth Dining.

## What I Learned

Quant + qual together beat either alone when stakeholders need confidence *and* empathy.
